From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Winter <danielwinterw@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 21:32:31 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] skippy: roll back staged verify windows in place

A recurrent verify window of up to n_rs_seq rows rolls back through the
rollback snapshots instead of a host checkpoint and replay, but staged
sessions were excluded on the grounds that they roll back across hosts.
They do not: each stage trims its own layers' recurrent state and
attention KV in its own context, to the same position as every other
stage. Split speculation therefore paid a full sequence-state copy, a
GPU sync and, on rejection, a second stage forward on every window.

What the snapshots cannot cover is a window that is no longer the last
decode of its sequence. Pipelined verification decodes the next window
before the previous one is resolved, and a trim may then land in the
earlier window or at the first, still speculative, row of the later
one. So a window rolls back in place only when it opens with nothing
else outstanding. If another decode lands on a window held in place,
its state after the first row is read from the snapshot that rolls back
to it and kept as a host checkpoint; the window's replay inputs are
recorded for that. The later window takes an ordinary checkpoint.

Retired checkpoints keep their buffers for the next one, so the
fallback path no longer allocates a full sequence state per window.

skippy_session_verify_rollback_counts reports how many of a session's
windows were held in place and how many in a host checkpoint since it was
reset, so a split can show which path each stage took. ABI 0.1.68.

This is its own patch rather than part of 0002: 0002 rolls back a
single-node session's window in place, while this owns the staged-session
rollback lifecycle (verify_window.cpp: when a window may roll back in
place, settling a window that a pipelined decode lands on into a host
checkpoint, buffer reuse) and its rollback-count ABI.
